<%# The bot's figure grid, right where a bot has it, in plain words. Six figures that tell ONE
    story: what went in, what it is worth, what it cost to trade, what has been banked, what is
    still riding, and the number the last two come to.
    Every one of them comes from `Tracker::Figures` — the templates used to work them out
    themselves, from two different truths, which is how they came to contradict each other with
    nothing able to notice. A figure that source cannot vouch for is a dash, and the banner above
    says which holding and why.
    All six are money and nothing else lives in this grid, so hiding balances takes the whole thing
    rather than emptying it. %>
<% unless current_user.hide_balances? %>
  <% figures = @figures %>
  <%# The value is known from the balances alone; everything the history decides waits for it. %>
  <% warming = figures&.ledger.nil? %>
  <% signed = ->(amount) { "#{'+' unless amount.negative?}#{@denomination.format(amount)}" } %>
  <% tone = ->(amount) { amount.negative? ? 'text-danger' : 'text-success' } %>
  <% money = ->(amount) { amount ? signed.call(amount) : '—' } %>
  <%# `--six` keeps the widest step at three columns below the point where all six fit on one row. %>
  <div class="widget data-grid data-grid--six">
    <%# What arrived without a purchase behind it is money in at what it was worth that day — the
        basis it carries — and the tile says how much, so it is not read as a claim it was all
        deposited. What each of those rows WAS is the record's business, row by row. %>
    <% received = figures&.ledger&.received_usd %>
    <%= render partial: 'bots/metrics_item', locals: {
      label: t('bot.details.stats.total_invested'),
      value: figures&.invested && @denomination.format(figures.invested),
      hint: (t('tracker.tiles.received_hint', received: @denomination.format(received)) if received&.positive?),
      loading: warming
    } %>
    <%= render partial: 'bots/metrics_item', locals: {
      label: t('bot.details.stats.portfolio_value'),
      value: figures && @denomination.format(figures.value),
      loading: figures.nil?
    } %>
    <%# What trading cost, in the quiet colour: a fact about the account, not an outcome to read as
        good or bad — and already deducted from the two P/L figures that follow. %>
    <%= render partial: 'bots/metrics_item', locals: {
      label: t('tracker.fees_paid'),
      value: figures&.fees && @denomination.format(figures.fees),
      loading: warming,
      value_class: 'data-grid__item__value--quiet'
    } %>
    <%= render partial: 'bots/metrics_item', locals: {
      label: t('bot.dca_index.realised_pnl'),
      value: figures&.realised && signed.call(figures.realised),
      loading: warming,
      value_class: figures&.realised && tone.call(figures.realised)
    } %>
    <%# A dash where a holding's basis cannot be stood behind — the banner names which. %>
    <%= render partial: 'bots/metrics_item', locals: {
      label: t('tracker.tiles.unrealised_pnl'),
      value: money.call(figures&.unrealised),
      loading: warming,
      value_class: figures&.unrealised && tone.call(figures.unrealised)
    } %>
    <%# The hint is the formula, in the grid's own words — it names the two tiles at the head of the
        same row, so it can be checked by looking rather than taken on trust. %>
    <%= render partial: 'bots/metrics_item', locals: {
      label: t('tracker.tiles.total_pnl'),
      hint: t('tracker.tiles.total_pnl_hint', value: t('bot.details.stats.portfolio_value'),
                                              invested: t('bot.details.stats.total_invested')),
      value: money.call(figures&.total),
      loading: warming,
      value_class: figures&.total && tone.call(figures.total)
    } %>
  </div>
<% end %>
